00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:05.060
JOE: How can we adapt our communication
style to meet the needs and preferences

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:06.790
of each individual with autism.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.599
BETH: When communicating with individuals
with autism it is important to remember

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:16.910
each person is unique and has unique needs
and preferences and these are not going

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:20.990
to be obvious straight away but there
are different approaches we can take.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.460
People should be aware of their
communication style and how

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:26.030
much spoken language they use.

00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:30.950
By modifying this using clear,
simplified instructions, or giving

00:00:30.969 --> 00:00:35.550
extra processing time before repeating
something or expecting an answer can help.

00:00:35.969 --> 00:00:40.440
JOE: Being specific with questions can
make answering easier or describing

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:44.050
objects specifically such as
including the size or the colour of

00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:46.379
something make it easier to identify.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:50.510
BETH: Giving instructions in order,
can help to make the process of doing

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:55.300
something much clearer, for example
when talking to a child by saying ‘First

00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:59.620
put your shoes on, then pick up your
bag’ this helps them to understand

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.350
the order in which to do things.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.459
JOE: Let’s now hear an
individuals own experience.

